\(\bullet\)**Is Negative Gluon Polarization Possible?**

As mentioned above, measurements conducted at RHIC have confirmed sizable positive gluon polarization inside the proton [50, 56]. However, the Jefferson Lab Angular Momentum (JAM) Collaboration [57] recently proposed that the gluon helicity distribution \(\Delta g\) is strongly dependent on the theoretical assumptions applied, namely the positivity constrain on the helicity distribution [50], SU(2) isospin symmetry and the SU(3) flavor symmetry. The left panel of Fig. 1.10 shows the \(\Delta g\) from the JAM Collaboration [57] with/without applying these constraints while the right panel of Fig. 1.10 presents the prediction of \(A_{LL}\) for inclusive jets for both positive and negative solutions of gluon helicity distributions, which shows that both predictions can describe the STAR measurements. Why does \(A_{LL}\) of inclusive jets allow a negative gluon \(\Delta g\)? One of the reason is that \(A_{LL}\) is proportional to the \(\Delta g^{2}\) in the dominant \(g\)-\(g\) scattering channel, which is not sensitive to the sign of \(\Delta g\). Some measurements conducted at RHIC, for example, the \(A_{LL}\) measurement of direct photons [58], which are mainly produced via the quark-gluon scattering at RHIC energy making \(A_{LL}\) a linear dependence on \(\Delta g\), and the di-jets \(A_{LL}\) measurements [51, 52, 44, 53, 54, 55] reducing parton scattering kinematics have shown their ability to distinguish between two scenarios of \(\Delta g\). Figure 1.11 shows the measured \(A_{LL}\) of direct photons at PHENIX and \(A_{LL}\) of di-jets at STAR. In Fig. 1.11, measured \(A_{LL}\) results disfavor the predictions from JAM negative \(\Delta g\).

In addition to these two measurements, the \(A_{LL}\) of jets tagged with charged pions carrying large momentum fraction of jets can shed light on the sign of the \(\Delta g\). The \(A_{LL}\) of \(\pi^{\pm}\)-tagged jets can be factorized as following:

\[A_{LL}=\frac{\sum_{ab}\Delta f_{a}\bigotimes\Delta f_{b}\bigotimes\mathrm{d} \hat{\sigma}^{f_{a}f_{b}\to f_{c}+X}\hat{a}_{LL}^{f_{a}f_{b}\to f_{c}+X} \bigotimes D_{f_{c}}^{\pi^{\pm}}}{\sum_{abc}f_{a}\bigotimes f_{b}\bigotimes \mathrm{d}\hat{\sigma}^{f_{a}f_{b}\to f_{c}+X}\bigotimes D_{f_{c}}^{\pi^{\pm }}}, \tag{1.9}\]

where \(D_{f_{c}}^{\pi^{\pm}}\) is the jet fragmentation function from parton \(f_{c}\) to \(\pi^{\pm}\). In the fragmentation processes, the \(u\) quark and \(d\) quark preferentially fragment into \(\pi^{+}\) and \(\pi^{-}\). As quark-gluon scattering is one of dominant subprocesses at RHIC energy, the \(\pi^{\pm}\) tagging not only enhances the \(u\)-\(g\) and \(d\)-\(g\) scattering but also suppresses the \(g\)-\(g\) scattering, which is blind in discrimination on the sign of \(\Delta g\). As we can see from the Fig. 1.6, helicity distributions of \(u\) quark and \(d\) quark have been well constrained and they are in opposite sign. Therefore, one would expect \(A_{LL}^{\pi^{+}}>0\) and \(A_{LL}^{\pi^{+}}>A_{LL}^{\pi^{-}}\) for positive \(\Delta g\) and the opposite case for negative \(\Delta g\). Figure 12 shows the \(A_{LL}\) measurements of inclusive \(\pi^{\pm}\) conducted at PHENIX Collaboration [59-60], and predictions with JAM positive and negative gluon \(\Delta g\)[57, 61] are compared. Though, the measurements are not precise to distinguish two \(\Delta g\) scenarios, the predictions clearly show that the \(\pi^{\pm}\)\(A_{LL}\) is sensitive to the sign of \(\Delta g\). In this thesis, more precise \(A_{LL}\) measurements of the \(\pi^{\pm}\)-tagged jets have been performed, and details about the measurements will be discussed in Chapter 3.

