## Chapter 5 Longitudinal Spin Transfer \(D_{ll}\) to \(\Lambda\) and \(\overline{\Lambda}\)

The Longitudinal spin transfer \(D_{LL}\) to \(\Lambda\) and \(\overline{\Lambda}\) in proton-proton collisions is expected not only to be sensitive to the helicity distributions of strange quark and anti-quark but also can shed light on polarized fragmentation functions. In this chapter, the analysis details will be presented.

### \(D_{ll}\) Extraction

#### 5.1.1 Extracting \(D_{ll}\) via the hyperon weak decay

The weak decay of \(\Lambda(\overline{\Lambda})\) guarantees that its polarization \(P_{\Lambda(\overline{\Lambda})}\) can be measured experimentally via its decay channel \(\Lambda\to p+\pi^{-}\) (\(\overline{\Lambda}\to\bar{p}+\pi^{+}\)) [70-72]. In the rest frame of \(\Lambda(\overline{\Lambda})\), the angular distribution of its decayed daughter proton (anti-proton) can be expressed as the following equation:

\[\frac{dN}{d\cos\theta^{*}}=\frac{AN_{0}}{2}\left(1+\alpha_{\Lambda(\overline{ \Lambda})}P_{\Lambda(\overline{\Lambda})}\cos\theta^{*}\right), \tag{5.1}\]

where \(\theta^{*}\) is the angle between the polarization direction of \(\Lambda(\overline{\Lambda})\) and the momentum of its daughter proton (anti-proton) in the rest frame of \(\Lambda(\overline{\Lambda})\). \(A\) is the detector acceptance, which varies with \(\theta^{*}\) and other variables. \(N_{0}\) is the total number of \(\Lambda(\overline{\Lambda})\) that decay to proton(anti-proton) and \(\pi^{-}(\pi^{+})\). The weak-decay parameter \(\alpha_{\Lambda}\) is taken to be \(\alpha_{\Lambda}=0.732\) [32] with \(\alpha_{\Lambda}=-\alpha_{\overline{\Lambda}}\) assuming no CP violation. In the \(D_{LL}\) measurement, the polarization direction is taken to be the momentum direction of the \(\Lambda(\overline{\Lambda})\) in the center-of-mass frame of the proton-proton collisions.

The details about the reconstruction of \(\Lambda\) hyperons are described in Chapter 4. In-jet hyperons are used for the \(D_{LL}\) analysis to study the helicity distribution of strange quark and anti-quark and polarized fragmentation functions. Figure 5.1 shows the 2D distribution of \(\cos\theta^{*}\) and the mass of the reconstructed \(\Lambda\) and \(\overline{\Lambda}\) candidates. Experimentally, the spin transfer \(D_{LL}\) is extracted from the asymmetry of the \(\Lambda\) hyperon yields with opposite beam helicity in a small \(\cos\theta^{*}\) interval via the followingequation [89-90]:

\[D_{LL}=\frac{1}{\alpha_{\Lambda(\overline{\Lambda})}P_{\rm beam}\ \langle\cos \theta^{*}\rangle}\frac{N^{+}-{\cal R}N^{-}}{N^{+}+{\cal R}N^{-}}, \tag{5.2}\]

where \(N^{+}\) and \(N^{-}\) are the yields of \(\Lambda\) hyperons with positive and negative beam helicity, respectively. \(P_{\rm beam}\) is the polarization of the proton beam and \(\langle\cos\theta^{*}\rangle\) is the average \(\cos\theta^{*}\) in that bin. In practice, \(\cos\theta^{*}\) is equally divided into 20 bins. \({\cal R}\) is the relative luminosity estimated with the VPD [109] and the ZDC [110]. The relative luminosities involved in the \(D_{LL}\) measurements are \(R_{4}\), \(R_{5}\) and \(R_{6}\). Details about these relative luminosities are in Sec. 2.3.3.

Equation (5.2) can be derived from Eq. (5.1). As its name implies, \(D_{LL}\) measures the spin transfer coefficient from the proton beam to \(\Lambda\) hyperon. In an other word, \(P_{\Lambda(\overline{\Lambda})}=D_{LL}P_{\rm beam}\). In a small \(\cos\theta^{*}\) interval, the detector acceptance \(A\) can be replaced with its average value:

\[\langle A(\cos\theta^{*})\rangle=\frac{\int A\left(\cos\theta^{*}\right)N(P, \cos\theta^{*})d\cos\theta^{*}}{\int N(P,\cos\theta^{*})d\cos\theta^{*}}, \tag{5.3}\]

Figure 5.1: The 2D distributions of \(\cos\theta^{*}\) vs hyperon invariant mass for \(\Lambda\).

where \(N(P,\cos\theta^{*})=\frac{N_{0}}{2}(1+\alpha P\cos\theta^{*})\), and \(P\) is the polarization of \(\Lambda\) hyperon. When the hyperon polarization is \(P\) and \(-P\), the hyperon yields in a small \(\cos\theta^{*}\) bin, \([\cos\theta_{1}^{*},\cos\theta_{2}^{*}]\), can be obtained by integrating the Eq. (5.1):

\[N(P)=\langle A(P,\cos\theta^{*})\rangle\frac{N_{0}}{2}\left\{ \cos\theta_{2}^{*}-\cos\theta_{1}^{*}+\frac{\alpha}{2}P\left(\cos^{2}\theta_{ 2}^{*}-\cos^{2}\theta_{1}^{*}\right)\right\} \tag{5.4}\] \[N(-P)=\langle A(-P,\cos\theta^{*})\rangle\frac{N_{0}}{2}\left\{ \cos\theta_{2}^{*}-\cos\theta_{1}^{*}-\frac{\alpha}{2}P\left(\cos^{2}\theta_{ 2}^{*}-\cos^{2}\theta_{1}^{*}\right)\right\} \tag{5.5}\]

The detector acceptance is independent of the \(\Lambda\) polarization when the bin width is small enough. Therefore, \(P\) can be obtained by solving the above two equations with the detector acceptance being canceled out:

\[P=\frac{1}{\alpha\left\langle\cos\theta^{*}\right\rangle}\frac{N(P)-N(-P)}{N(P )+N(-P)}. \tag{5.6}\]

Flipping the beam polarization will inverse the polarization of \(\Lambda\) hyperons. Therefore, \(N(P)\) and \(N(-P)\) correspond to the \(\Lambda\) hyperon yields with positive and negative beam helicity, respectively. The Eq. (5.2) can be obtained by dividing the beam polarization from Eq. (5.6) and the relative luminosity \(matchalR\) in Eq. (5.2) is used to correct the imbalance of the luminosity in the sampling from different beam helicity.

In the \(D_{LL}\) measurements, only one beam need to be polarized. The single-spin yields of \(\Lambda\) hyperons can be obtained by combining the double-spin yields weighted with the corresponding relative luminosities. At each \(\cos\theta^{*}\) bin, the \(D_{LL}\) can be extracted with Eq. (5.7) and (5.8) assuming the polarized beam is blue beam and yellow beam, respectively.

\[D_{LL} =\frac{1}{\alpha_{\Lambda(\overline{\Lambda})}P_{B}\left\langle \cos\theta^{*}\right\rangle}\frac{(N^{++}/R_{4}+N^{+-}/R_{6})-(N^{-+}/R_{5}+N^{ --})}{(N^{++}/R_{4}+N^{+-}/R_{6})+(N^{-+}/R_{5}+N^{--})} \tag{5.7}\] \[D_{LL} =\frac{1}{\alpha_{\Lambda(\overline{\Lambda})}P_{Y}\left\langle \cos\theta^{*}\right\rangle}\frac{(N^{++}/R_{4}+N^{-+}/R_{5})-(N^{+-}/R_{6}+N^{ --})}{(N^{++}/R_{4}+N^{-+}/R_{5})+(N^{+-}/R_{6}+N^{--})} \tag{5.8}\]

In this analysis, \(D_{LL}\) is measured as a function of \(\Lambda\) hyperons \(p_{T}\) and jet momentum fraction \(z\equiv(p_{\Lambda}\cdot p_{jet})/|p_{jet}|^{2}\) carried by \(\Lambda\) hyperons as measuring \(D_{LL}\) vs \(z\) provides direct probe to polarized fragmentation functions according to Ref. [92].

The residual backgrounds under the hyperon mass peak are estimated with the side-band method. At each \(p_{T}\) or \(z\) bin, the \(D_{LL}^{raw}\) under the mass peak and the \(D_{LL}^{bkg}\) from the residual background are obtained by averaging over all \(\cos\theta^{*}\) bins. The influence of the residual background on the \(D_{LL}\) is subtracted with the following equation:

\[D_{LL}=\frac{D_{LL}^{raw}-rD_{LL}^{bkg}}{1-r}, \tag{5.9}\]

where \(r\) is the residual background fraction under the mass peak, estimated with the side-band method. Figure 5.2, as examples, presents the \(D_{LL}^{raw}\) as a function of \(\cos\theta^{*}\) in each \(p_{T}\) bins with the blue beam polarized for positive pseudo-rapidity region \(0<\eta_{\Lambda}<1.2\), where \(\eta_{\Lambda}\) is defined as the pseudo-rapidity relative to the polarized beam direction. Similar results of \(D_{LL}^{raw}\) for \(\Lambda\) is shown in Fig. 5.3.

In principle, the \(D_{LL}\) results extracted with blue beam polarized and yellow beam polarized should be consistent with each other. The final \(D_{LL}\) results are the statistical average of the results for blue beam and yellow beam. Figure 5.4, as an example, shows the comparison of the \(D_{LL}\) results between blue beam and yellow beam. As we can see in Fig. 5.4, results between blue beam and yellow beam are consistent. The combined results for blue beam and yellow beam \(\Lambda\) and \(\overline{\Lambda}\) are presented in Fig. 5.5.

